def burn_rate(errors, requests, slo):
    return (sum(errors) / sum(requests)) / (1 - slo)

def should_page(errors, requests, slo=0.999, threshold=14.4):
    long_burn = burn_rate(errors[-60:], requests[-60:], slo)
    short_burn = burn_rate(errors[-5:], requests[-5:], slo)
    return long_burn > threshold and short_burn > threshold

exercises.solution:
The long window makes the alert *significant* — only a burn that has spent a meaningful share of the budget (14.4 × 1 h ≈ 2% of a 30-day budget) can trip it. The short window makes it *current* — once the problem stops, the 5-minute burn rate drops within minutes and the page stops, instead of staying on for the rest of the hour.

```python
def burn_rate(errors, requests, slo):
    return (sum(errors) / sum(requests)) / (1 - slo)

def should_page(errors, requests, slo=0.999, threshold=14.4):
    long_burn = burn_rate(errors[-60:], requests[-60:], slo)
    short_burn = burn_rate(errors[-5:], requests[-5:], slo)
    return long_burn > threshold and short_burn > threshold
```

In the tests, the three-minute blip has a 5-minute burn rate of about 60 but a 60-minute burn rate of about 5.5, so it no longer pages; the outage that ended half an hour ago has a 60-minute burn rate of about 150 but a 5-minute one below 1, so it no longer pages either. Production rules add a second pair (6 hours and 30 minutes at a burn rate of 6) for slower burns, and a ticket for very slow ones.
